{"causes": ["The ambitious expansionist policies of Philip II and Alexander the Great created a vast empire requiring loyal military and administrative personnel.", "Alexander's sudden death in 323 BCE triggered a power vacuum and intense rivalries among his generals, known as the Diadochi.", "The inherent instability of a rapidly expanded empire with diverse cultural and political elements necessitated strong, loyal administrators like Aristonous."], "effects": ["Aristonous's execution by Cassander solidified the latter's control over Macedon and further destabilized the remnants of the Argead dynasty.", "His death served as a stark warning to other officials loyal to Alexander's family, accelerating the consolidation of power by the Diadochi.", "The elimination of figures like Aristonous contributed to the complete fragmentation of Alexander's empire into independent Hellenistic kingdoms."], "relationships": [{"sourceSlug": "alexander-the-great", "sourceName": "Alexander the Great", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "aristonous-of-pella", "targetName": "Aristonous of Pella", "context": "Aristonous served as one of Alexander's Somatophylakes (bodyguards) and a loyal general during his campaigns from 335-323 BCE."}, {"sourceSlug": "aristonous-of-pella", "sourceName": "Aristonous of Pella", "verb": "PARTICIPATES_IN", "targetSlug": "wars-of-the-diadochi", "targetName": "Wars of the Diadochi", "context": "Aristonous was a key participant in the early conflicts following Alexander's death, aligning with Perdiccas and Eumenes against other Diadochi."}, {"sourceSlug": "aristonous-of-pella", "sourceName": "Aristonous of Pella", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "pella-greece", "targetName": "Pella, Greece", "context": "Aristonous was born in Pella, the ancient capital of Macedon, and remained connected to its political landscape throughout his career."}, {"sourceSlug": "cassander", "sourceName": "Cassander", "verb": "CAUSES", "targetSlug": "aristonous-of-pella", "target